CommitDate: Tue Sep 22 13:10:24 2026 -0500

    fix(install): prune stale installed files on upgrade; cap rules .bak retention (TJ-GAP-072)
    
    The documented upgrade path is re-running install.sh, but the lib-tree
    step was a bare `cp -R` over the installed tree: files deleted from the
    checkout persisted in the installed tree forever, and differing user-rule
    backups (.bak-<utc>) accumulated unbounded.
    
    - install.sh records an `.install-manifest` (sorted relative paths of
      every regular file the checkout ships) inside the installed tree and,
      on re-install, prunes exactly the previously-manifested files the new
      checkout no longer carries. User-added files and anything outside
      plugin/terminal_jail are never touched; manifest write is atomic.
    - differing-rule rules backups are capped: the newest 5 are kept, older
      ones pruned with an announce line each (UTC names make plain sort
      order == chronological order).
    - plugin/test_install.py: regression tests install from a scratch
      checkout, delete a real module, re-install and assert the stale file
      is pruned (stdout names it), an unmanifested user file survives, and
      a first-ever install prunes nothing; the retention test runs 7
      differing-rule installs and asserts exactly the newest 5 backups
      remain with exactly 2 prune announcements. _INSTALL_TOOLS gains
      find/sort/sed for the new pipeline.
    
    Salvaged from a worker session that died mid-task (dead pre-commit, work
    recovered from the tree; foreman fixed the test's 7-5 arithmetic (2
    prunes, not 3) and added the missing `sed` stub that the curated-PATH
    tests needed for the new manifest pipeline).
    
    Co-authored-by: Alexis Okuwa <wojonstech@gmail.com>
